<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ubaid period</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 6500–3800 BCE</span> · Also known as Ubaid culture</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ubaid period is a prehistoric period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, from about 6500 to 3800 BCE, named after Tell al-Ubaid near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>. Farming villages spread across southern Mesopotamia, using irrigation and producing distinctive painted pottery, and the first temples, as at Eridu, appeared. Ubaid culture spread north and along the Gulf, and the period led into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk-period">Uruk period</a>, when the first cities emerged.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ubaid-period.jpg" alt="Ubaid period" width="320" height="170" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bowl_MET_DP104228_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settlement began in the <b>Ubaid period</b>, around 3800 BCE by one reckoning, in marshy surroundings where fish, birds, tubers and reeds could feed a town without irrigation, and the canals that cross the site were probably used for transport.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It grew from two settlements of the <b>Ubaid period</b>, Kullaba and Eanna, which merged during the fourth millennium BCE into a single city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chiefdoms arose independently from Mesopotamia, where many archaeologists place the first in the <b>Ubaid period</b>, to the Americas, where Cahokia grew after about 1050 into the largest chiefly centre north of Mexico, and to Polynesia, where Kamehameha I ruled all the Hawaiian Islands as king by 1810.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote>Eridu was settled around 5400 BCE on a sandy rise at the edge of the marshes near the ancient shore of the Gulf, in the <b>Ubaid period</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/eridu">Eridu</a></li><li><blockquote>Nippur was inhabited from the <b>Ubaid period</b> and by the Early Dynastic period had become the religious heart of Sumer.</blockquote><a href="/state/nippur">Nippur</a></li></ul></section>
